<h1>Activated Roller Belt (ARB)</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">An Activated Roller Belt (ARB) is a modular plastic belt with small rollers embedded across its surface. Products ride on the rollers. When the rollers are driven from below, they move the product sideways or ahead of the belt, which lets one conveyor divert, sort, singulate, align, merge, and turn products without pusher arms or pop-up wheels above the conveying surface. <a href="https://www.intralox.com/products/arb-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intralox</a> describes ARB as a patented technology and sells ARB belts and equipment for <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">diverting and sorting</a> in parcel, e-commerce, and <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> applications.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How ARB Works</h2>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/arb-intralox-aligner.webp" width="1000" height="667" alt="Sealed cardboard cases travel in a line along one side of a gray plastic belt studded with small rollers, on a stainless conveyor with gearmotors mounted on the side." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Cases lined up against one side of an Intralox ARB aligner. The angled rollers in the belt move each case sideways as the belt carries it forward. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.intralox.com/products/arb-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intralox</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>The Roller Mechanism</h3>
<p>Each belt module carries small rollers on axles, set at a fixed angle to the direction of belt travel. Intralox's Series 400 Angled Roller belts use 2.0-inch spacing between rollers and come with rollers at <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-400/0-degree-angled-rollertm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">0 degrees</a> (in line with travel), <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-400/30-degree-angled-rollertm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">30 degrees</a>, or 90 degrees. Intralox states that custom belts with any combination of 0, 30, 45, or 60 degree rollers are available.</p>
<p>Products rest on the rollers, not on a flat belt top. Intralox describes the result plainly: when the belt rollers are in motion, product moves faster than the belt, and when the rollers do not rotate, product travels at belt speed.</p>
<h3>Activation</h3>
<p>Beneath the belt, an activation surface contacts the underside of the rollers where product should move. As the belt passes over it, friction spins the rollers. A spinning angled roller pushes the product along the roller's direction while the belt carries it forward, so the product moves diagonally. Where the rollers are not engaged, the product continues straight at belt speed.</p>
<p>Activation can be placed at any point along the belt and across any portion of its width. A wide ARB conveyor can activate the left side, right side, center, or full width, and different sections along its length can serve different destinations.</p>
<h3>Roller Angle and Function</h3>
<p>The roller angle sets the direction of the push. Intralox positions its 0-degree belt for creating gaps between items with minimal lateral movement, its 30-degree belt for aligning, box turning, and passive discharges, and its <a href="https://www.intralox.com/belt-finder/modular-plastic-belting/series-400/90-degree-angled-rollertm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">90-degree belt</a> for merges and 90-degree transfers. Higher angles move the product sideways in less conveyor length. The right angle depends on the function, product size, and available length.</p>
<h3>Product Spacing</h3>
<p>Pusher diverters and pop-up wheel diverters must retract before the next product arrives, so they need a gap between products. ARB functions with fixed activation, such as aligning against a guide or singulating a mass flow, act on every product that passes and can handle touching or randomly spaced product. Selective sorting is different: the activated section switches on for one package, so packages still need enough space that only the target package is over that section. Confirm the minimum gap with the supplier for your product mix and rate.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">ARB Applications</h2>
<p>Intralox lists ARB equipment for sorting, singulating and descrambling, merging, gapping, 90-degree transferring, aligning, switching, diverting, and case turning.</p>
<h3>Diverting &amp; Sorting</h3>
<p>ARB routes products to multiple destinations. One ARB belt with activation at different positions can divert products to several takeaway lanes. Upstream scanners read barcodes and the controls switch activation for each package's destination. This is common in parcel and e-commerce distribution and in <a href="/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/">reject systems</a> on packaging lines.</p>
<h3>Singulation</h3>
<p>Singulation separates products from a mass flow (random, overlapping, touching) into a single-file stream with gaps between products. ARB singulation uses a wide belt with angled activation that spreads products apart and steers them into a single lane. It can replace multi-stage singulation built from speed-differential conveyors and guides.</p>
<h3>Alignment</h3>
<p>ARB alignment uses angled rollers to push products against a side guide, squaring them to the belt edge. Products that arrive at random positions on a wide belt exit aligned for downstream scanners, labelers, and case sealers that need product in a consistent position.</p>
<h3>Merge</h3>
<p>ARB merge combines two or more incoming streams into one. Products from side-feed conveyors enter the ARB belt, and angled roller activation steers them into the main flow. Compare merge rate and density against the mechanical merge it would replace.</p>
<h3>Orientation (90-Degree Rotation)</h3>
<p>ARB can turn products by engaging rollers under one end of the product while the other end continues at belt speed. This rotates cases and cartons from long-side-leading to short-side-leading (or the reverse) without a mechanical turntable. Product rotation is needed before case sealers, stretch wrappers, <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">laning stations</a>, and palletizers that require a specific orientation.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/arb-intralox-case-turner.webp" width="1000" height="750" alt="Three white cartons at different angles on a gray roller-studded plastic belt, next to a side-mounted unit with a gearmotor." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Cartons at different orientations on an Intralox ARB case turner. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.intralox.com/products/arb-equipment" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Intralox</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="compare-heading">ARB vs Traditional Diverters</h2>
