</table>
<p>Ask each supplier for wear parts, maintenance intervals, and expected downtime so you can compare lifetime cost, not just purchase price. See the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for cost factors.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<h3>Belt Width &amp; Length</h3>
<p>ARB belt width must accommodate the widest product plus the divert path. The product must travel diagonally from its starting position to the takeaway point within the activated length, so wider belts and lower roller angles need more length. Suppliers lay out belt width and length for your product sizes and functions. Intralox Series 400 Angled Roller belts have a 2.00-inch pitch and a 6-inch minimum width.</p>
<h3>Product Bottom Surface</h3>
<p>ARB rollers move the product through contact with its bottom, so products need a bottom that rests on and engages the rollers. Soft or loose-fill bags may sag between rollers instead of being steered. Intralox notes that product behavior varies with shape, weight, conveyor design, and belt speed. Test unfamiliar products before committing to a layout.</p>
<h3>Activation Control</h3>
<p>Activation can be fixed (always engaged) or switchable (engaged and released by actuators). Fixed activation suits functions where every product is treated the same, such as aligning or a dedicated lane split. Switchable activation suits sorting, where only selected products divert based on barcode scans or other criteria. Switchable activation adds actuators, controls, and tracking logic.</p>
<h3>Speed &amp; Throughput</h3>
<p>Higher belt speeds increase throughput but leave less time for the rollers to complete the divert. Larger, heavier products need more activated length or lower speed. Ratings vary by equipment: <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/3200-series/3200-activated-roller-belt" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner</a> rates its 3200 Series ARB conveyor at speeds up to 150 feet per minute, widths from 8 to 60 inches, and products as small as 6 by 6 inches. Ask suppliers for rated throughput with your product mix.</p>
<h3>Roller Material and Accumulation</h3>
<p>Check roller material against the duty. Intralox notes that its black polyurethane rollers are not recommended for product accumulation conditions, so do not plan to queue product on an ARB section without confirming the belt is suited to it.</p>
<h3>Integration with Existing Systems</h3>
<p>ARB conveyors integrate with upstream barcode scanners, vision systems, and conveyor controls for sorting. They hand off to downstream <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a>, <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyors</a>, chutes, and <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zones</a>. Define who supplies the controls and sort logic when ARB equipment comes from one vendor and the surrounding conveyor from another.</p>
<h2 id="industries-heading">Industry Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Parcel distribution:</strong> ARB sorters and singulators handle the wide variety of parcel sizes, shapes, and weights in parcel sort centers. Singulation converts bulk induction into single-file flow for scanning, and diverts route packages to destination lanes or chutes.</p>
<p><strong>E-commerce fulfillment:</strong> E-commerce warehouses use ARB to sort outbound orders to shipping lanes by carrier, service level, or region. The mix of polybags, padded mailers, small boxes, and large cases in these order streams suits equipment that does not need adjustment for each product.</p>
<p><strong>Food and beverage packaging:</strong> ARB conveyors sort and turn cases of food and beverage products ahead of palletizing. Steering rather than pushing reduces case damage. Confirm that belt and roller materials suit the environment and any food-contact requirement.</p>
<p><strong>Retail distribution:</strong> Retail distribution centers use ARB to sort cases and cartons to store-specific lanes. One ARB sorter can replace several mechanical diverters along a trunk line, reducing equipment count and floor space.</p>
